**Version Used**:

- Originally observed with Roslyn `5.12.0-1.26452.1`, commit `e42c0e42e89e41522593d0816707e71781ecbee2`, bundled with VS Code C# extension `2.160.4`.
- The original cache-poisoning sequence also reproduces with separately built compiler assemblies from Roslyn main snapshot `dc3a633f11ebc89bc9e4fed553747b3c36ee5f52`.
- The initial fix in [#85935](https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/pull/85935), head `5d7c696b4b2b8052e701f0de4cacfb5b1be47b21`, fixes that sequence but still reproduces the filtered-consumer/parse-options case below. This issue tracks the underlying filtering contract and its regression coverage, not only cache construction.

**Steps to Reproduce**:

1. Create a console project using the project file and `Program.cs` below. The three generators have distinct types: a post-init marker producer, a pre-compilation producer, and a regular-phase consumer of `CompilationProvider`.
2. Set `RoslynAssemblyDir` to a directory containing a matching `Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.dll` and `Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp.dll` from the affected payload or a repository build. This avoids substituting the SDK compiler for the assemblies used by the generator driver. The example targets .NET 10 and can be built with an appropriate .NET 10 or newer SDK.
3. Run:

```powershell
dotnet run --project .\GeneratorFilterRepro.csproj -p:RoslynAssemblyDir="C:\path\to\matching\roslyn-assemblies"
```

The primary sequence is **full run -> method-body-only user edit -> filtered run selecting only the pre-compilation generator -> full run on the identical edited input `Compilation` instance**. The program also includes both required controls and a separate case that changes preprocessor symbols and runs the consumer during the filtered pass.

`GeneratorFilterRepro.csproj`:

```xml
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <LangVersion>preview</LangVersion>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);RSEXPERIMENTAL007</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <Reference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis">
      <HintPath>$(RoslynAssemblyDir)\Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.dll</HintPath>
    </Reference>
    <Reference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp">
      <HintPath>$(RoslynAssemblyDir)\Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp.dll</HintPath>
    </Reference>
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
```

`Program.cs`:

```csharp
using System.Collections.Immutable;
using System.Text;
using System.Text.Json;
using Microsoft.CodeAnalysis;
using Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp;
using Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.Text;

var results = new[]
{
    RunCase("no-filter-control", usePreCompilation: true, filteredPass: false),
    RunCase("ordinary-output-control", usePreCompilation: false, filteredPass: true),
    RunCase("filtered-precomp", usePreCompilation: true, filteredPass: true),
    RunCase("filtered-consumer-after-options-change", usePreCompilation: true,
        filteredPass: true, changeParseOptions: true)
};
Console.WriteLine(JsonSerializer.Serialize(results, new JsonSerializerOptions { WriteIndented = true }));

static CaseResult RunCase(
    string name, bool usePreCompilation, bool filteredPass, bool changeParseOptions = false)
{
    var options = new CSharpParseOptions(LanguageVersion.Preview);
    var original = CSharpSyntaxTree.ParseText(
        "public class UserCode { public int Value() => 1; }", options);
    var compilation = CSharpCompilation.Create(
        "GeneratorFilterRepro",
        [original],
        [MetadataReference.CreateFromFile(typeof(object).Assembly.Location)],
        new CSharpCompilationOptions(OutputKind.DynamicallyLinkedLibrary));
    var marker = new MarkerGenerator().AsSourceGenerator();
    var preCompilation = new PreCompilationGenerator(usePreCompilation).AsSourceGenerator();
    var consumer = new MarkerConsumer().AsSourceGenerator();
    GeneratorDriver driver = CSharpGeneratorDriver.Create(
        [marker, preCompilation, consumer], parseOptions: options);
    driver = driver.RunGeneratorsAndUpdateCompilation(
        compilation, out _, out var initialDiagnostics);
    if (!initialDiagnostics.IsEmpty)
        throw new InvalidOperationException("Initial run must succeed.");

    var edited = original.WithChangedText(SourceText.From(
        "public class UserCode { public int Value() => 2; }", Encoding.UTF8));
    if (changeParseOptions)
    {
        options = options.WithPreprocessorSymbols("UPDATED");
        edited = edited.WithRootAndOptions(edited.GetRoot(), options);
        driver = driver.WithUpdatedParseOptions(options);
    }
    compilation = compilation.ReplaceSyntaxTree(original, edited);

    var filteredDiagnostics = ImmutableArray<Diagnostic>.Empty;
    if (filteredPass)
    {
        driver = driver.RunGenerators(compilation, context =>
            context.Generator == preCompilation
            || (changeParseOptions && context.Generator == consumer));
        filteredDiagnostics = driver.GetRunResult().Diagnostics;
    }

    // Use the identical edited input Compilation for the filtered and full operations.
    driver = driver.RunGeneratorsAndUpdateCompilation(
        compilation, out var output, out var finalDiagnostics);
    return new CaseResult(
        name,
        filteredDiagnostics.Select(diagnostic => diagnostic.Id).ToArray(),
        finalDiagnostics.Select(diagnostic => diagnostic.Id).ToArray(),
        output.GetTypeByMetadataName("PostInitMarker") is not null,
        output.GetDiagnostics().Count(diagnostic => diagnostic.Severity == DiagnosticSeverity.Error));
}

sealed record CaseResult(
    string Name, string[] FilteredDiagnostics, string[] FinalDiagnostics,
    bool FinalCompilationHasMarker, int CompilerErrors);

sealed class MarkerGenerator : IIncrementalGenerator
{
    public void Initialize(IncrementalGeneratorInitializationContext context) =>
        context.RegisterPostInitializationOutput(output =>
            output.AddSource("Marker.g.cs", "public class PostInitMarker { }"));
}

sealed class PreCompilationGenerator(bool usePreCompilation) : IIncrementalGenerator
{
    public void Initialize(IncrementalGeneratorInitializationContext context)
    {
        if (usePreCompilation)
        {
            context.RegisterPreCompilationSourceOutput(context.ParseOptionsProvider, (output, _) =>
                output.AddSource("Pre.g.cs", "public class PreCompilationMarker { }"));
        }
        else
        {
            context.RegisterSourceOutput(context.ParseOptionsProvider, (output, _) =>
                output.AddSource("Pre.g.cs", "public class PreCompilationMarker { }"));
        }
    }
}

sealed class MarkerConsumer : IIncrementalGenerator
{
    private static readonly DiagnosticDescriptor s_missing = new(
        "POSTINIT_MISSING", "Missing post-init source", "CompilationProvider lost PostInitMarker",
        "Repro", DiagnosticSeverity.Error, isEnabledByDefault: true);

    public void Initialize(IncrementalGeneratorInitializationContext context) =>
        context.RegisterSourceOutput(context.CompilationProvider, (output, compilation) =>
        {
            if (compilation.GetTypeByMetadataName("PostInitMarker") is null)
                output.ReportDiagnostic(Diagnostic.Create(s_missing, Location.None));
        });
}
```

**Diagnostic Id**:

`POSTINIT_MISSING` is the custom diagnostic emitted by the repro, not a built-in analyzer diagnostic. Its message is `CompilationProvider lost PostInitMarker`.

**Expected Behavior**:

- All initial runs succeed.
- Regular-phase consumers can resolve current shared post-init and pre-compilation types, including during filtered runs and after parse-option changes.
- The filter controls regular source, implementation, and host execution, not the initialization/shared-source phases needed by other generators. Pre-compilation evaluation should remain incremental; excluded regular callbacks must not execute.
- The cached compilation contains the exact shared syntax trees represented by its identity. The consumer's input and final output must not disagree about whether `PostInitMarker` exists.
- Both controls produce no generator diagnostics. The final output contains `PostInitMarker` and has no compiler errors.

**Actual Behavior**:

Results from the exact same repro source against three separately staged, matching compiler assembly sets:

| Case | Unfixed main snapshot `dc3a633f` | Initial PR head `5d7c696b` | Local phase-level revision |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Pre-compilation output, no intervening filtered pass | Pass | Pass | Pass |
| Filtered pass, ordinary output instead of pre-compilation | Pass | Pass | Pass |
| Filtered pre-compilation-only pass, then full on the same edited compilation | Final generator diagnostics contain `POSTINIT_MISSING` | Pass | Pass |
| Parse-options change, filtered pre-compilation producer + consumer, post-init producer excluded | Filtered diagnostics contain `POSTINIT_MISSING` | Filtered diagnostics contain `POSTINIT_MISSING` | Pass |

**Every row still returns a final output compilation containing `PostInitMarker` with zero compiler errors.** The disagreement is in the compilation supplied to the consumer, not the final compilation's validity. The initial full run succeeds in every case.

### Root cause and contract

In the original implementation, `GeneratorDriver.RunGeneratorsCore` skips an excluded generator before collecting its post-init trees into the intermediate compilation. Later cache construction records the retained shared trees from all generator states. With pre-compilation output present, a cache miss can therefore store a compilation missing a tree claimed by its key, and the next full pass can reuse it.

Reconstructing cache misses from the key repairs that immediate mismatch, but does not ensure shared-source freshness: skipped producers are still not initialized/reparsed, or their pre-compilation outputs remain stale. Excluding retained post-init trees whose options need updating prevents incompatible tree options but causes selected consumers to lose shared types. [The review of #85935](https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/pull/85935#pullrequestreview-5510555987) identifies the broader requirement: shared phases should participate independently of regular execution filtering.

This is reachable through the IDE's `CreateOnlyRequired` policy: only Razor is currently classified as required, and Razor uses pre-compilation output. It is not necessary to run MAUI, VS Code, Visual Studio, or Hot Reload to reproduce the compiler inconsistency.

### Related consistency cases covered by the local revision

The broader correction also needs to protect these closely coupled cases:

- First-ever filtered runs must initialize shared producers; parse-option and additional-file changes must refresh their shared trees without invoking excluded regular callbacks.
- Resuming a regular pipeline downstream of cached shared providers must not classify a newly created output as cached or crash incremental-step tracking.
- A changed shared hint must not publish two sources with the same hint for one generator alongside retained regular output; a later hint reversal must restore the retained regular source without forcing its callback.
- Retained diagnostics must not point to syntax trees removed/replaced in the current published output. Temporary shared-phase failure/recovery must not irreversibly lose the last regular result or its previous failure.
- Replacing a generator with another that emits the same `SourceText` instance/hint/options at the same index must not reuse a compilation containing the previous generator's different syntax tree. Ordered actual tree identity avoids that aliasing.

The local revision retains compilation-reference reuse and incremental pre-compilation caching. It does not disable the cache or add public API signatures. The runnable repro above was verified before filing; broader local validation of the prepared revision passed 47,740 cases with zero failures and 284 existing/platform skips across compiler/workspace suites on .NET 10 and targeted source-generation suites on .NET Framework 4.7.2. No quantified performance improvement or product-debugger fix is claimed.

Related: [#85935](https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/pull/85935); [#83089](https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/issues/83089) defines the pre-compilation API.

