## Overview

Fixes #56428 by restoring `dotnet watch` browser refresh on static SSR pages in Blazor Web apps configured for WebAssembly or Auto interactivity. The client already owns browser-tools activation, but its old runtime-only entry point never runs when the page does not start WebAssembly. One commit (`6379a03ffb`) changes two JavaScript runtime files, one test file, and three guidance files; the governing constraint is **connect before WebAssembly starts without forcing it to start, losing deferred edits, or creating another connection/keypair**.

## Design

**Activate at the Web lifecycle boundary, not only the runtime boundary.** The client initializer exports `afterWebStarted(): Promise<void>` to check provider availability and import its existing app-hosted configuration. Its existing `onRuntimeConfigLoaded` / `onRuntimeReady` activation and runtime debug gate remain intact. This avoids restoring HTML injection or adding a competing host initializer: the [existing target condition](https://github.com/dotnet/sdk/blob/6379a03ffb3835849f7c6d7ce19525c8cf88f5ea/src/StaticWebAssetsSdk/Targets/Microsoft.NET.Sdk.StaticWebAssets.HotReload.targets#L60-L68) deliberately suppresses the host's assets when it references a WebAssembly client. Configuration-module caching and the browser client's existing injection sentinel continue to absorb duplicate activation.

**Retain updates on the existing shared readiness object.** `globalThis.__DOTNET_WATCH_HOT_RELOAD_AGENT` already carries `ready`, `setReady`, and the SDK agent's apply APIs. This change adds a synchronous `isReady` marker and an optional `pendingUpdates` array of `{ deltas, responseLoggingLevel }`. Only the client-owned early Web initializer creates that array. Initialization replay and later live updates use the same FIFO; acknowledging an update before runtime startup now means it has been **retained**, rather than silently skipped.

| Runtime path | Readiness boundary |
|---|---|
| SDK agent on .NET 10+ | The existing `onRuntimeReady` initializes the SDK agent and releases readiness in `finally`, including initialization failure. |
| Legacy agent on .NET 8/9 | The new `afterWebAssemblyStarted(): void` releases readiness after Blazor has configured its interop dispatcher. Merely finding a legacy apply function is too early. |
| No early client-owned Web activation | No deferred queue is created. Standalone WASM and server-only activation retain their existing direct-apply / bounded replay-wait behavior. |

There are **no new CLI options, MSBuild properties, provider messages, replay endpoints, or key owners**. The connection must keep processing SSR refreshes while WebAssembly is absent; delaying an acknowledgement until runtime startup would block that flow. Deferred apply failures are therefore reported in the browser console, not through an unsolicited second socket acknowledgement.

The diagram groups the application forwarding route and watch provider as **App / watch**. The initializer, browser client, and runtime communicate within the same browser document. Auto shares the SSR activation path shown here; its initial interactive component can instead run on the server without starting WASM.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant B as Browser / Blazor Web
    participant I as Client initializer
    participant C as Browser-tools client
    participant H as App / watch
    participant R as WASM runtime / agent

    B->>H: Load static Home
    H-->>B: SSR HTML and Blazor Web bootstrap
    B->>I: afterWebStarted()
    I->>I: Create pendingUpdates before any await, unless already ready
    I->>H: Check app-hosted provider settings
    H-->>I: Hot Reload enabled
    I->>C: Import existing app-hosted configuration
    C->>H: Open authenticated browser-tools connection
    H-->>C: InitializeSession with earlier updates
    C->>C: Retain replay records in FIFO order
    C-->>H: Acknowledge retained snapshot
    Note over B,R: Home remains static SSR. WASM has not started.

    opt Managed code is edited while viewing SSR
        H-->>C: ApplyManagedCodeUpdates
        C->>C: Append live updates to the same FIFO
        C-->>H: Acknowledge retention without waiting for WASM
        opt Server-rendered content changed
            H-->>C: RefreshBrowser
            C->>B: Blazor._internal.hotReloadApplied()
            B->>H: Enhanced navigation request
            H-->>B: Updated SSR HTML
        end
    end

    B->>R: Navigate to a WASM-interactive component
    alt SDK agent on .NET 10+
        R->>I: onRuntimeReady(runtime)
        I->>R: Initialize SDK agent and publish apply APIs
        I->>I: setReady() in finally
    else Legacy agent on .NET 8/9
        R->>I: afterWebAssemblyStarted()
        Note over I,R: Blazor interop dispatcher is now configured
        I->>I: setReady()
    end
    I-->>C: ready resolves after isReady becomes true
    C->>C: Detach pendingUpdates before applying
    loop Replay records followed by buffered live updates
        C->>R: applyDeltas()
    end
    Note over I,C: A late Web-start callback cannot recreate the queue
    H-->>C: Subsequent live managed update
    C->>R: Apply directly, preserving running component state
```

## Implementation

The entry point establishes buffering **before its settings request can race with runtime startup**. If the runtime is already ready, it must not create a queue that has missed its drain opportunity.

```javascript
// src\WasmSdk\Sdk\HotReload\Microsoft.NET.Sdk.WebAssembly.HotReload.lib.module.js.template
export async function afterWebStarted() {
    const agent = hotReloadAgentSignal();
    if (!agent.isReady) {
        agent.pendingUpdates ??= [];
    }

    if (!await isHotReloadEnabled()) {
        return;
    }

    // Reuse the existing client configuration, keypair, and connection path.
    await import(new URL(configModulePath, import.meta.url).href);
}

// Legacy apply wrappers can exist before their interop dispatcher is usable.
export function afterWebAssemblyStarted() {
    hotReloadAgentSignal().setReady();
}
```

Both modules keep the same rendezvous-helper contract because either can create the shared object first. The synchronous marker is needed in addition to the promise: a Web-start callback must decide immediately whether buffering is still appropriate, without recreating it after readiness.

```javascript
// src\WasmSdk\Sdk\HotReload\Microsoft.NET.Sdk.WebAssembly.HotReload.lib.module.js.template
// Inside hotReloadAgentSignal(); the browser-tools module has the matching implementation.
if (!agent.ready) {
    agent.ready = new Promise(resolve => {
        agent.setReady = () => {
            agent.isReady = true;
            resolve();
        };
    });
}
```

Both the authenticated initialization snapshot and live managed-update handler already converge on `applyDeltas`. The new branch retains the original delta objects and logging level; it does not serialize and reconstruct them or introduce a second apply implementation. The session initializer skips its bounded apply-API wait when this queue exists, so SSR refresh messages are not held behind runtime startup.

```javascript
// src\StaticWebAssetsSdk\Targets\HotReload\HotReload-browser-tools.js
function applyDeltas(deltas, responseLoggingLevel) {
  const agent = hotReloadAgentSignal();
  if (agent.pendingUpdates) {
    agent.pendingUpdates.push({ deltas, responseLoggingLevel });
    console.debug('Managed code updates deferred until WebAssembly starts.');
    return [];
  }

  // Existing SDK / modern Blazor / legacy Blazor apply dispatch follows unchanged; omitted.
}
```

After a successful session initialization, the client registers the drain **even if no queue exists yet**. At readiness it detaches the queue before calling `applyDeltas`, preventing those calls from re-enqueuing themselves. The loop has no `await`, so buffered updates drain in order before later socket messages can interleave; paths without a queue do nothing.

```javascript
// src\StaticWebAssetsSdk\Targets\HotReload\HotReload-browser-tools.js
// After initializeSession() succeeds and completeInitialization() releases normal message handling.
const agent = hotReloadAgentSignal();
agent.ready.then(() => {
  const updates = agent.pendingUpdates;
  delete agent.pendingUpdates;
  if (!updates) {
    return;
  }

  if (updates.length && !hasDeltaApplyApi()) {
    console.error('Unable to apply deferred Hot Reload updates because the WebAssembly Hot Reload agent is unavailable.');
    return;
  }

  for (const update of updates) {
    try {
      const log = applyDeltas(update.deltas, update.responseLoggingLevel);
      // Returned entries are written to console.error / console.warn / console.debug by severity; omitted.
    } catch (error) {
      console.error('Unable to apply deferred Hot Reload updates.', error);
    }
  }
});
```

The build regression test verifies that the hosted manifest exposes the **client-owned, primary, build-only initializer**, the host's JS-module manifest includes it, its generated Web-start body establishes buffering before the asynchronous settings check, and no competing host `HotReload` directory is produced. Existing enabled-build rows also assert both new exports; disabled-build coverage remains in place. The architecture and area guidance describe this same ownership and readiness flow.

